    "rust-collections-ask": {
      "id": "rust-collections-ask",
      "title": "Reason: why Entry beats two lookups",
      "kind": "question",
      "mode": "review",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "rust-collections"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Count how many times each approach asks the map whether the key exists."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Believing entry is only a performance trick, when it also changes which borrows stay alive.",
        "Thinking contains_key followed by insert is rejected because the key type is not Copy."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "You total rows by owner and want to add to an existing total or start one. Two calls look obvious, but the first holds a borrow that the second needs to end."
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "It clones the key once per row, which is faster than hashing twice.",
        "It answers presence and hands back the slot in one lookup.",
        "It sorts the keys while inserting, so the totals can be printed in order.",
        "It refuses duplicate keys, which keeps a total from being written twice."
      ],
      "answer": 1,
      "explanation": "entry looks the key up once and returns either a vacant slot to fill or an occupied one to update. Two calls hash the key twice and hold a borrow across both, which is exactly the shape the compiler rejects when you then insert. The Entry API is not about speed alone, because it states the operation as one decision. Sorting and duplicate refusal are properties of other collections, not of entry."
    },
